1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,184
Mom! Come in.

2
00:00:02,434 --> 00:00:05,484
Welcome, Ruth. We kept the afternoon free.

3
00:00:05,734 --> 00:00:09,991
Then I have brought good company and a small
surprise.

4
00:00:10,241 --> 00:00:14,498
Come sit by us. The surprise can wait a
minute.

5
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,129
Six packing cubes, one for each of you.

6
00:00:19,379 --> 00:00:22,585
My socks are receiving their own apartments.

7
00:00:22,835 --> 00:00:24,772
They need addresses first.

8
00:00:25,022 --> 00:00:29,497
Then you children can help the apartments find
their owners.

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:34,707
Once, Dan packed a tiny sock instead of his
necktie.

10
00:00:34,957 --> 00:00:37,176
A very compact fashion choice.

11
00:00:37,426 --> 00:00:39,645
Did it match your shirt?

12
00:00:39,895 --> 00:00:44,499
I never got far enough to try it under my
collar.

13
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,607
Your suitcase can go on this stand.

14
00:00:48,857 --> 00:00:51,964
The top drawer is empty for you.

15
00:00:52,214 --> 00:00:54,501
You have thought of everything.

16
00:00:54,751 --> 00:00:59,498
Good. There's room for you to unpack at your
own pace.

17
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,343
This checklist keeps my own things together.

18
00:01:03,593 --> 00:01:05,311
An excellent traveling companion.

19
00:01:05,561 --> 00:01:09,529
We will leave your lavender pouch just as it
is.

20
00:01:09,779 --> 00:01:14,498
Thank you. I like finding that one just where
I put it.

21
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:19,359
We noticed before he left. He borrowed a
proper tie.

22
00:01:19,609 --> 00:01:22,739
So the sock did not attend the meeting?

23
00:01:22,989 --> 00:01:24,660
It had other appointments.

24
00:01:24,910 --> 00:01:29,499
I hope it found a shoe with a less formal
dress code.

25
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:35,651
Use the cubes and borrow my checklist idea. I
will rest next door.

26
00:01:35,901 --> 00:01:37,488
Of course, Mom.

27
00:01:37,738 --> 00:01:40,234
We will keep your room peaceful.

28
00:01:40,484 --> 00:01:44,497
Thanks, girls. I'll look forward to hearing
how it went.

29
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:48,116
Thank you for the cubes, Grandma.

30
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:52,573
You are welcome. Tell me how the new
apartments work.

31
00:01:52,823 --> 00:01:55,042
We will, after your rest.

32
00:01:55,292 --> 00:01:59,499
I already have a pair of tenants in mind,
Grandma.

33
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:03,283
These socks are clean and dry.

34
00:02:03,533 --> 00:02:06,739
And these cubes still have no names.

35
00:02:06,989 --> 00:02:09,772
Let us sort before we store.

36
00:02:10,022 --> 00:02:14,497
Good. A gray cube doesn't tell you much by
itself.

37
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:18,197
I can see my green heels.

38
00:02:18,447 --> 00:02:20,734
A whole family of socks.

39
00:02:20,984 --> 00:02:24,501
Six pairs. We will check the owners together.

40
00:02:24,751 --> 00:02:29,498
I'll know those green bits even with my eyes
half shut.

41
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:34,198
Six pairs and six places. I can manage that.

42
00:02:34,448 --> 00:02:38,918
One pair in each. Surely I cannot mix up
identical boxes.

43
00:02:39,168 --> 00:02:41,322
Green heels in this one.

44
00:02:41,572 --> 00:02:44,498
There. Its neighbor gets the next pair.

45
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,514
That zipper went all the way around.

46
00:02:48,764 --> 00:02:52,573
They are packed. The names can catch up later.

47
00:02:52,823 --> 00:02:55,439
Which one had the green heels?

48
00:02:55,689 --> 00:02:59,498
Ah, they're all snug. I'll sort out names
later.

49
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:02,900
Now where should these go?

50
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:07,270
Packing cubes, suitcase. That seems a very
strong connection.

51
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:09,920
Well, look who brought them.

52
00:03:10,170 --> 00:03:14,290
Grandma's case has exactly the right sort of
space.

53
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:17,900
There's room on this side.

54
00:03:18,150 --> 00:03:20,980
Six small apartments, one large building.

55
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:23,200
That's all six together.

56
00:03:23,450 --> 00:03:27,140
The lavender pouch can have its own side.

57
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:32,900
There. The case closes nicely.

58
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:36,410
Everything put away. A remarkably tidy
afternoon.

59
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:39,490
Funny how quiet the counter looks.

60
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:43,430
Wait till everybody sees how quickly I
finished.

61
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:47,470
Where are the socks?

62
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:49,520
Safely packed.

63
00:03:49,770 --> 00:03:51,570
For which destination?

64
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:55,940
Well, that depends on whose suitcase we're
talking about.

65
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:04,190
My socks are not in their new apartment.

66
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:06,410
Perhaps the building moved.

67
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:09,060
I may have arranged transportation.

68
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:14,290
Did you tell my feet? They didn't pack for a
trip.

69
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:17,900
The labels never went anywhere.

70
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:21,840
Then let us ask the person who packed.

71
00:04:22,090 --> 00:04:24,060
That would be me.

72
00:04:24,310 --> 00:04:28,860
Which way did you go after you closed the
zippers?

73
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:35,594
I put our six cubes in Ruth's suitcase. I
assumed they were hers.

74
00:04:35,844 --> 00:04:37,937
Thank you for telling us.

75
00:04:38,187 --> 00:04:40,655
Then we know where to look.

76
00:04:40,905 --> 00:04:44,498
Good thing she hasn't taken that suitcase
anywhere yet.

77
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:49,837
We can retrieve our gifts and leave Grandma's
pouch alone.

78
00:04:50,087 --> 00:04:52,784
Then describe, ask, label, and store.

79
00:04:53,034 --> 00:04:55,321
Four steps. No moving buildings.

80
00:04:55,571 --> 00:04:59,498
That's right. We'll only move what belongs to
us.

81
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:03,760
This is exactly where I left them.

82
00:05:04,010 --> 00:05:06,410
Open the main compartment gently.

83
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:09,060
The lavender pouch stays closed.

84
00:05:09,310 --> 00:05:13,430
Got it. I can see the two areas now.

85
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:19,707
Gray cubes on the left. Lavender pouch on the
right.

86
00:05:19,957 --> 00:05:22,971
An address I should have checked earlier.

87
00:05:23,221 --> 00:05:25,837
Which side are we reaching into?

88
00:05:26,087 --> 00:05:29,498
Just the gray side. Her pouch stays put.

89
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:33,330
One, two, three, four, five, six.

90
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:35,380
Six gifts recovered.

91
00:05:35,630 --> 00:05:38,460
And nobody needed a boarding pass.

92
00:05:38,710 --> 00:05:43,260
And all six came back on the very same trip.

93
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:48,330
My new travel-size socks. Very economical.

94
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:50,550
Those are mine, Dad.

95
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:53,630
Let us check, rather than guess.

96
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:57,570
Okay, Max. What gives this particular pair
away?

97
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:04,309
Mine have green heels, and both socks fit me.

98
00:06:04,559 --> 00:06:07,175
Excellent. My feet were being ambitious.

99
00:06:07,425 --> 00:06:10,041
Back to the clean sorting counter.

100
00:06:10,291 --> 00:06:14,498
Good. I'd rather my socks stayed the size they
are.

101
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:18,718
Do we need to sort Grandma's pouch too?

102
00:06:18,968 --> 00:06:21,436
No. Those are her private things.

103
00:06:21,686 --> 00:06:24,904
We have only our own cubes to fix.

104
00:06:25,154 --> 00:06:29,497
So we can help her best by leaving that one
closed.

105
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:35,050
Describe the pair, ask its owner, then add a
name.

106
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:37,700
Before I invent a destination.

107
00:06:37,950 --> 00:06:39,920
That order sounds promising.

108
00:06:40,170 --> 00:06:44,290
Exactly. No cube goes anywhere till its owner
agrees.

109
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:49,975
We can borrow the method without copying
Grandma's personal list.

110
00:06:50,225 --> 00:06:52,162
Six names, six pairs.

111
00:06:52,412 --> 00:06:54,772
And we check each one.

112
00:06:55,022 --> 00:06:59,497
Good. We'll write down what belongs to our own
family.

113
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:02,470
No suitcase this time.

114
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:05,550
Good. My socks prefer staying home.

115
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,630
Let us start at this end.

116
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:13,430
I'll look at the heels before we guess about
sizes.

117
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:19,017
There, that's the last thing back in place.

118
00:07:19,267 --> 00:07:23,604
Your own things are right where you left them,
Mom.

119
00:07:23,854 --> 00:07:26,141
I'll leave the pouch closed.

120
00:07:26,391 --> 00:07:29,498
Sleep well. The sorting can wait outside.

121
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:32,900
The room is tidy again.

122
00:07:33,150 --> 00:07:35,980
Now we finish our own sorting.

123
00:07:36,230 --> 00:07:38,630
One owner at a time.

124
00:07:38,880 --> 00:07:43,430
Good. I want to know whose pair we're holding
first.

125
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:47,900
What makes this pair yours?

126
00:07:48,150 --> 00:07:50,550
Green heels and my size.

127
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:52,600
Evidence beats enthusiasm.

128
00:07:52,850 --> 00:07:57,400
Look, both heels are green. I checked the
other one.

129
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:02,300
Two matching socks.

130
00:08:02,550 --> 00:08:04,350
Both mine.

131
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:07,000
Then the owner is settled.

132
00:08:07,250 --> 00:08:11,800
Good. The pair has a name before the cube
does.

133
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:18,911
Name on the cube, not on the sock.

134
00:08:19,161 --> 00:08:20,982
My toes appreciate that.

135
00:08:21,232 --> 00:08:24,643
And it can be read without opening it.

136
00:08:24,893 --> 00:08:29,497
Then I can find it even before school in the
morning.

137
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:32,300
Mine, checked, closed.

138
00:08:32,550 --> 00:08:35,380
The first apartment has an owner.

139
00:08:35,630 --> 00:08:38,890
Can you read my name from there?

140
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:42,400
Sure can. No need to unzip anything.

141
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:47,470
These have coral stars.

142
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:50,120
A famous constellation of toes.

143
00:08:50,370 --> 00:08:52,170
Who recognizes them?

144
00:08:52,420 --> 00:08:56,540
I do. I picked that pattern for a reason.

145
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:03,760
They are mine. I chose the stars.

146
00:09:04,010 --> 00:09:05,980
Yes, and both match.

147
00:09:06,230 --> 00:09:09,060
My green ones are already home.

148
00:09:09,310 --> 00:09:13,860
And my stars can have their own place next
door.

149
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:17,470
Ella, on the handle.

150
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:19,520
Not Grandma's suitcase.

151
00:09:19,770 --> 00:09:21,570
Correct destination, too.

152
00:09:21,820 --> 00:09:26,370
Good. I want to find them without taking a
trip.

153
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:32,900
Orange heels, longer than Max's.

154
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:34,950
Those are mine.

155
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:38,030
Your feet have been busy growing.

156
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:42,830
That's how I can spot them beside his little
pair.

157
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:48,330
May I label this cube Leo?

158
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:50,980
Yes. That is my pair.

159
00:09:51,230 --> 00:09:54,490
A short question saves a long search.

160
00:09:54,740 --> 00:09:58,860
Good. Now I can find them without opening
Max's.

161
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:02,300
Checked and zipped.

162
00:10:02,550 --> 00:10:04,350
Three owners found.

163
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:06,570
And no departures scheduled.

164
00:10:06,820 --> 00:10:11,370
Three more at home, then. We're halfway down
the row.

165
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:17,900
Plum dots. These are mine.

166
00:10:18,150 --> 00:10:20,120
I nearly said Nina.

167
00:10:20,370 --> 00:10:23,630
Nearly is a good time to ask.

168
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:28,000
Maya, are the plum dots the ones you picked?

169
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:32,470
I chose the dots.

170
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:34,690
Mine are plain teal.

171
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:38,630
Thank you. Two people, two answers worth
hearing.

172
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:44,290
We can see why guessing would've put them in
the wrong cube.

173
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:47,470
Maya. Plum dots. Confirmed.

174
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:49,690
And safely stored together.

175
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:52,340
And whose name faces out?

176
00:10:52,590 --> 00:10:56,280
Mine. I can read it from right here.

177
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,330
These are the plain teal ones.

178
00:11:03,580 --> 00:11:05,980
Yes, that is my pair.

179
00:11:06,230 --> 00:11:09,060
The description arrived before the guess.

180
00:11:09,310 --> 00:11:13,430
Good. That saves somebody opening the wrong
cube tomorrow.

181
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:18,330
Would six identical pairs be easier?

182
00:11:18,580 --> 00:11:21,410
Not for telling whose is whose.

183
00:11:21,660 --> 00:11:23,630
Different can be useful.

184
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:28,430
Good thing my heels are green. They're quicker
to spot.

185
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:32,300
Nina, checked.

186
00:11:32,550 --> 00:11:34,350
Thank you.

187
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:37,860
Five pairs, five names. One to go.

188
00:11:38,110 --> 00:11:42,660
Good. I think the last one's pretty easy to
recognize.

189
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:47,470
Navy stripes. Largest size.

190
00:11:47,720 --> 00:11:49,690
Those are definitely mine.

191
00:11:49,940 --> 00:11:51,740
Much less travel-sized.

192
00:11:51,990 --> 00:11:56,540
Thank goodness. I couldn't get a toe into
Max's pair.

193
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:03,330
Suitable for feet. Unsuitable for meetings.

194
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:05,980
We will tell your collar.

195
00:12:06,230 --> 00:12:08,200
No sock tie today.

196
00:12:08,450 --> 00:12:13,430
Good. My neck and my feet each get the right
outfit.

197
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:18,330
Dan. Navy stripes. Staying at home.

198
00:12:18,580 --> 00:12:20,980
That is a complete address.

199
00:12:21,230 --> 00:12:23,200
Six out of six.

200
00:12:23,450 --> 00:12:28,430
Great. Now let's give all six an address we
can find.

201
00:12:30,500 --> 00:12:32,900
Six names, with no duplicates.

202
00:12:33,150 --> 00:12:35,550
Now choose the storage places.

203
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:38,630
Then we can find them again.

204
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:43,430
Somewhere that makes sense when we're looking
in a hurry.

205
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:47,900
Six pairs means twelve socks.

206
00:12:48,150 --> 00:12:49,950
Twelve feet?

207
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:53,030
Six people, two feet each. Fortunately.

208
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:57,830
Good. None of those twelve has to travel by
itself.

209
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:02,900
No lonely socks left here.

210
00:13:03,150 --> 00:13:05,120
All twelve accounted for.

211
00:13:05,370 --> 00:13:08,200
Then the repair really is finished.

212
00:13:08,450 --> 00:13:13,860
Good. The basket bottom was the last place I
wanted to check.

213
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:18,197
Should we count one more time?

214
00:13:18,447 --> 00:13:20,734
We checked each pair already.

215
00:13:20,984 --> 00:13:24,911
Now we keep the record and move them once.

216
00:13:25,161 --> 00:13:29,498
Okay. I'd rather find mine than count them all
again.

217
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:33,283
Adults across the top, children below.

218
00:13:33,533 --> 00:13:35,893
Everyone can reach without climbing.

219
00:13:36,143 --> 00:13:39,349
A building with an excellent floor plan.

220
00:13:39,599 --> 00:13:44,497
Then keep the names facing this way as they go
in.

221
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:48,760
From where we stand: Dan, Maya, Nina.

222
00:13:49,010 --> 00:13:51,410
And Leo, Ella, Max underneath.

223
00:13:51,660 --> 00:13:53,630
Same viewpoint. Clear directions.

224
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:58,860
Good. It matters which way you're standing
when you say left.

225
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:02,900
Mine is in the middle.

226
00:14:03,150 --> 00:14:05,550
Mine is on the right.

227
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:09,060
And I finally know where mine lives.

228
00:14:09,310 --> 00:14:13,860
And all three labels face the person doing the
looking.

229
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:17,300
Left.

230
00:14:17,550 --> 00:14:19,350
Middle.

231
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:22,000
Right. My apartment is downstairs.

232
00:14:22,250 --> 00:14:27,230
Good. Face the shelf and you can point to your
own.

233
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:33,607
We can read them before opening anything.

234
00:14:33,857 --> 00:14:36,144
That will help tomorrow too.

235
00:14:36,394 --> 00:14:39,501
A useful label explains, then stays put.

236
00:14:39,751 --> 00:14:44,498
Tomorrow I'll only have to open the one with
my name.

237
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:48,330
Even the marker has a place.

238
00:14:48,580 --> 00:14:50,980
Does it need a suitcase?

239
00:14:51,230 --> 00:14:53,030
Only its cap.

240
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:57,400
Good. It wouldn't have much to wear on
vacation.

241
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:04,129
This can wait for the next clean load.

242
00:15:04,379 --> 00:15:07,162
Empty, correctly located, and not traveling.

243
00:15:07,412 --> 00:15:11,041
Shall we leave it here by the washer?

244
00:15:11,291 --> 00:15:14,497
Yes. That'll make the next load easier.

245
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:17,300
Mine is bottom-right.

246
00:15:17,550 --> 00:15:19,520
Mine is beside yours.

247
00:15:19,770 --> 00:15:23,890
And mine is on the left. No search needed.

248
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:28,690
I like directions I can use before I'm fully
awake.

249
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:32,654
A clean towel for Mom.

250
00:15:32,904 --> 00:15:35,830
She said the top drawer was free.

251
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:39,778
Let us keep the rest just as it is.

252
00:15:40,028 --> 00:15:44,498
Right. She gets to choose what goes in her own
things.

253
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:48,330
The towel goes in this drawer?

254
00:15:48,580 --> 00:15:51,410
Yes. That is the guest drawer.

255
00:15:51,660 --> 00:15:54,060
You checked the destination first.

256
00:15:54,310 --> 00:15:58,860
I like asking before I have to carry it back.

257
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:02,470
One towel, top drawer.

258
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:04,690
A very uneventful delivery.

259
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:06,910
Sometimes that is perfect.

260
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:11,710
Especially if you're trying to give somebody a
quiet afternoon.

261
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:18,760
The blanket was already where it belongs.

262
00:16:19,010 --> 00:16:22,270
I can admire that without packing it.

263
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:24,320
Excellent restraint.

264
00:16:24,570 --> 00:16:29,120
Thanks. A folded blanket doesn't really need a
new address.

265
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:34,017
We did not find any pajamas to hang.

266
00:16:34,267 --> 00:16:36,554
They are Grandma's to unpack.

267
00:16:36,804 --> 00:16:39,501
Helping includes leaving her a choice.

268
00:16:39,751 --> 00:16:44,498
So she can hang them herself if she feels like
it.

269
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:47,900
Grandma's list goes back here.

270
00:16:48,150 --> 00:16:50,980
Our own record comes with us.

271
00:16:51,230 --> 00:16:53,630
Two lists, two clear owners.

272
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:58,430
Good. Grandma won't have to hunt for her own
list.

273
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:02,470
Hands washed after sorting.

274
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:05,550
Even though the socks were clean?

275
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:09,490
Yes. We are finished with the laundry now.

276
00:17:09,740 --> 00:17:13,860
Good. My hands can retire from sock duty now.

277
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:18,283
Your questions made the sorting easy.

278
00:17:18,533 --> 00:17:21,739
And your shelf plan made it useful.

279
00:17:21,989 --> 00:17:24,349
I benefited from both departments.

280
00:17:24,599 --> 00:17:29,497
So tomorrow morning nobody has to ask where
their socks went.

281
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:35,104
I wanted to make the visit easy. I moved too
fast.

282
00:17:35,354 --> 00:17:37,573
You fixed it with us.

283
00:17:37,823 --> 00:17:40,439
Next time, ask before the suitcase.

284
00:17:40,689 --> 00:17:44,498
Agreed. Even a quick question would've saved
two trips.

285
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:48,330
Our socks almost went on vacation.

286
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:51,410
A journey of approximately one hallway.

287
00:17:51,660 --> 00:17:54,920
And a return ticket paid in questions.

288
00:17:55,170 --> 00:17:59,290
Next time they're staying here without buying
a ticket.

289
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:02,900
A peaceful table at last.

290
00:18:03,150 --> 00:18:05,550
With everyone's feet properly represented.

291
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:08,630
Mine are still under my chair.

292
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:13,860
My stars are off duty. They know where to go
tomorrow.

293
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:19,309
Did Grandma tease you about that tie for
years?

294
00:18:19,559 --> 00:18:22,573
Kindly. Then she helped me find one.

295
00:18:22,823 --> 00:18:25,439
A family tradition: notice, then help.

296
00:18:25,689 --> 00:18:29,498
I'm glad the family remembers the helping part
too.

297
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:33,646
The label worked because we asked first.

298
00:18:33,896 --> 00:18:35,633
Exactly.

299
00:18:35,883 --> 00:18:39,443
A name cannot make the wrong pair yours.

300
00:18:39,693 --> 00:18:44,498
Good. My stars won't turn into dots behind a
different name.

301
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:48,812
Top row for adults. Bottom row for us.

302
00:18:49,062 --> 00:18:51,988
Mine is bottom-right when I face it.

303
00:18:52,238 --> 00:18:55,164
Directions with a starting point. Very
helpful.

304
00:18:55,414 --> 00:18:59,498
Exactly. Turn toward the shelf first and the
directions work.

305
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:04,129
Another day, we can organize the garden pots.

306
00:19:04,379 --> 00:19:07,162
Do plants need name labels too?

307
00:19:07,412 --> 00:19:09,772
A question for another afternoon.

308
00:19:10,022 --> 00:19:14,497
I wonder whether Dad will give the pots
apartments too.

309
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:19,129
Grandma does not need a label, does she?

310
00:19:19,379 --> 00:19:21,739
She introduced herself years ago.

311
00:19:21,989 --> 00:19:24,772
And remembers my packing history perfectly.

312
00:19:25,022 --> 00:19:29,497
Good. That story was worth bringing along in
her suitcase.

313
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:34,129
Six pairs at home. One suitcase correctly
packed.

314
00:19:34,379 --> 00:19:37,162
And one generous guest resting peacefully.

315
00:19:37,412 --> 00:19:39,772
That is a good afternoon.

316
00:19:40,022 --> 00:19:44,497
And she'll wake up to a room that's still
hers.

317
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:49,757
Next time I help, I will ask where things
belong.

318
00:19:50,007 --> 00:19:52,655
That sounds like a wonderful welcome.

319
00:19:52,905 --> 00:19:54,589
Especially for socks.

320
00:19:54,839 --> 00:19:59,498
Even better if you ask before you close the
zipper, Dad.
